.TH PNCH 1 "November 2026" "pnch 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pnch \- clock in and out from the terminal, log what you did, export a weekly log + invoice PDF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pnch
.br
.B pnch
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pnch
keeps a log of the time you work and what you did in each block.
At the end of a week you review and
.I approve
it, which locks the week, and then export a PDF with a log page and an invoice page.
.PP
Run
.B pnch
on its own in a terminal to open the full-screen app.
Every action is also available as a
.B pnch
.I command
for scripts, status bars and quick use from the shell.
When output is not a terminal, plain
.B pnch
prints the dashboard instead.
.PP
Everything is written to disk the moment it changes.
Weeks run Monday to Sunday; a block that runs past midnight counts on the day it started.
.SH THE APP
Type into the prompt bar at the bottom:
.TP
.BR in " [\fIitem\fR]"
Punch in, optionally with the first thing you are working on.
.TP
.B out
Punch out. Shows what you did, asks "Did you do anything else not listed?",
lets you adjust the day and times, then saves the block.
.TP
.BR undo " [\fIn\fR], " redo " [\fIn\fR]"
Take back the last change (or put it back).
.TP
.BR /vday " [\fIday\fR]"
View a day: its blocks, total and a timeline bar. Enter on a block edits it;
the last row, \fB+ add a block\fR, adds one.
.TP
.BR /vweek " [\fIweek\fR]"
View a week's blocks with a timeline per day.
.TP
.BR /weeks
A calendar of all your weeks. Up/down picks one;
\fBp\fR previews, \fBe\fR exports, \fBa\fR approves, Enter opens it.
.TP
.BR /previewLogs " [\fIweek\fR]"
Preview the week's log + invoice PDF (inline in kitty, Ghostty and WezTerm; otherwise your PDF viewer).
.TP
.BR /exportLogs " [\fIweek\fR]"
Save the PDF into the export folder. An unapproved week is approved first.
.TP
.B /config
Hourly rate, color theme, export folder and file-name style.
.TP
.B /help
All commands and keys.
.PP
Typing \fB/\fR opens a menu of these; arrow keys pick one, Tab completes, Enter runs it.
What you are working on goes in the separate \fIworking on\fR box.
.PP
If a block's times overlap another block you are offered to merge them, fit the new block
into the free gap, shorten the other block, or replace it.
.SS Keys
.TP 18
.B tab
Switch between the prompt bar and the top section.
.TP
.B arrows
In the top section, move between the working-on box, its items, the today panel
and the week panel. Enter on a panel opens it.
.TP
.B ctrl+s
Punch in / punch out.
.TP
.B ctrl+n
Jump to the working-on box.
.TP
.BR ctrl+z ", " ctrl+y
Undo, redo.
.TP
.B ctrl+p
Command palette (approve, export, past blocks, themes, ...).
.TP
.B ctrl+t
Fix the last block's times.
.TP
.BR ctrl+l ", " f5
Week log, PDF preview.
.TP
.B ctrl+b
Show or hide the side panels.
.TP
.BR f2 ", " delete
Rename or remove the selected item.
.TP
.B f1
All shortcuts.
.TP
.B ctrl+q
Quit. Always asks first; when you are on the clock you can punch out or keep the clock running.
.SH COMMANDS
Week arguments (\fB\-w\fR) accept \fBthis\fR, \fBlast\fR, \fB\-2\fR, or any date inside the week.
Days accept \fBtoday\fR, \fByesterday\fR, weekday names, \fB10/14\fR, \fB10/14/26\fR or \fB2026\-10\-14\fR.
Times accept \fB9\fR, \fB9am\fR, \fB9:30pm\fR, \fB930pm\fR, \fB21:30\fR, \fBnoon\fR and \fBmidnight\fR.
.TP
.BR in " [\fIitem\fR...] [\fB\-\-at\fR \fItime\fR]"
Clock in.
.TP
.BR out " [\fIitem\fR...] [\fB\-\-at\fR \fItime\fR]"
Clock out. Without an item it shows the block and asks for anything missing.
.TP
.BR note " \fItext\fR... [\fB\-\-id\fR \fIn\fR]"
Add a line to the current (or last) block.
.TP
.BR add " \fIday start end\fR [\fIitem\fR...]"
Log a block after the fact. An end before the start means past midnight.
.TP
.BR edit " \fIn\fR [\fB\-\-date\fR] [\fB\-\-start\fR] [\fB\-\-end\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR]... [\fB\-\-add\-note\fR]..."
Change a block. With no options it asks interactively.
.TP
.BR rm " \fIn\fR [\fB\-y\fR]"
Delete a block.
.TP
.BR log " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
List a week's blocks with their numbers.
.TP
.BR status " [\fB\-W\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
Dashboard; \fB\-W\fR keeps it live.
.TP
.BR weeks " [\fB\-n\fR \fIcount\fR]"
Recent weeks with hours, amount, invoice number and status.
.TP
.BR preview " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIpage\fR] [\fB\-o\fR]"
Show the PDF, watermarked PREVIEW until the week is approved. \fB\-o\fR opens your PDF viewer.
.TP
.BR approve " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
Review a week, confirm billed hours, the invoice line, number and issue date, then lock it.
.TP
.BR unapprove " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
Unlock an approved week so it can be edited.
.TP
.BR export " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR] [\fB\-\-out\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-o\fR]"
Write the PDF for an approved week into the export folder (or \fIpath\fR).
.TP
.BR undo " [\fIn\fR], " redo " [\fIn\fR]"
Undo or redo changes. The last 100 are kept.
.TP
.BR config " [\fB\-e\fR] [\fB\-\-path\fR]"
Show the config, open it in \fB$VISUAL\fR/\fB$EDITOR\fR, or print where files live.
.TP
.BR bar " [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
One-line status for waybar, polybar, tmux or a shell prompt.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fI~/.config/pnch/config.toml\fR is created on first run. Sections:
.TP
.B [you]
name, title, address lines, email, paypal (shown on the invoice).
.TP
.B [client]
name, address lines, email.
.TP
.B [invoice]
doc_title, role, rate, first_number, due_business_days,
billing (\fBdown\fR, \fBnearest\fR, \fBup\fR, \fBquarter\fR or \fBexact\fR), footer.
.TP
.B [export]
dir, and filename, a pattern using \fB{start}\fR, \fB{end}\fR (10_12),
\fB{start_iso}\fR, \fB{end_iso}\fR (2026\-10\-12), \fB{number}\fR, \fB{name}\fR and \fB{year}\fR.
.TP
.B [ui]
theme (\fBcatppuccin\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBgruvbox\fR, \fBtokyonight\fR, \fBrosepine\fR),
banner and mascot (\fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pnch/config.toml
Your details, rate, theme and export settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/pnch/data.json
The log. \fIdata.json.bak\fR is the previous version; a damaged file is replaced by it automatically.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/pnch/undo/ ", " redo/
Undo history.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/pnch/session.json
Heartbeat used to notice that pnch closed unexpectedly while you were clocked in.
.TP
.I ~/Documents/pnch/
Default export folder.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PNCH_HOME
Keep config, data and cache in this one folder instead (handy for testing).
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME
Respected when \fBPNCH_HOME\fR is not set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command is refused (for example clocking in twice), 2 when an argument
cannot be read (for example a time like \fB25pm\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pnch in fixing the scraper
pnch note "checked the VPN prefix list"
pnch out --at 5:15pm
pnch add yesterday 11pm 12:12am pulled the leads list together
pnch approve -w last && pnch export -w last
pnch bar --json
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/ricozama121803/pnch
